# Discovery — Handoff to csdd

This is the seam between discovery and development. It converts the PRD's intent
into csdd's validated artifacts: always-on steering memory plus a spec whose
requirements pass the EARS gate. After this, `wf:development` can start
architecture with full, mechanically-checked context.

## Goal

Translate `docs/product/prd.md` into csdd steering updates and a spec seeded with
EARS-formatted requirements that pass `csdd spec validate`, so the downstream
workflow inherits a clean, gated starting point.

## Default Operation Mode

Direct execution, driven by the `csdd` CLI. You never hand-edit `spec.json`,
frontmatter, or task annotations — every artifact is created via `csdd` and only
its prose body is filled in. The deliverable is a passing `csdd spec validate`.

## Collect Inputs First

- Is `docs/product/prd.md` complete and checklist-passing? (run `discovery-prd`
  validate first if unsure)
- What is the feature slug for this work? (kebab-case, e.g. `photo-albums`)
- Does the workspace have baseline steering yet? (`csdd steering list`)

## Execution Workflow

### 1) Update steering (always-on memory)
- If no baseline exists, run `csdd steering init` (or `csdd init --with-baseline`).
- Fill `product.md` from the PRD's Vision, Target User, and Success Metrics.
- If `discovery-research` produced technical findings, fold the durable
  constraints into `tech.md`. Only capture decisions, not the whole research.
- Run `csdd steering validate`.

### 2) Initialize the spec
- `csdd spec init <feature>` — creates `specs/<feature>/spec.json`.
- `csdd spec generate <feature> --artifact requirements` — scaffolds
  `requirements.md` from the template.

### 3) Translate PRD → EARS requirements
- For each PRD `FR-N`, write a requirement in `requirements.md` using EARS:
  `WHEN <trigger>, the system SHALL <observable behavior>` (or WHILE / IF / WHERE
  forms). Consult `.claude/rules/ears-format.md`.
- Carry each PRD user journey (`UJ-N`) into the acceptance narrative so
  traceability survives the handoff.
- Never use "should" — EARS uses SHALL. One requirement per behavior.

### 4) Gate it
- `csdd spec validate <feature>` — fix every reported issue (it checks EARS
  syntax, unique headers, no "should").
- `csdd spec approve <feature> --phase requirements` once validation is clean.

### 5) Announce the handoff
- Report the spec name and confirm requirements are approved. Point the user at
  `wf:development` → `dev-architecture` as the next step.

## Gotchas

- Do not bypass the gates with `--force`. If validation fails, fix the
  requirement text, not the gate.
- Steering is durable memory, not a PRD dump. Capture the decisions a future
  contributor must know, not the full narrative — the PRD stays the source.
- The PRD lives in `docs/product/`; the spec lives in `specs/`. Keep them
  separate — the spec references the PRD, it does not duplicate it.
- One `FR-N` may map to one or more EARS requirements, but every EARS requirement
  must trace back to a `FR-N`. No orphan requirements.

## Verification Before Reporting

- Run: `csdd steering validate`, then `csdd spec validate <feature>`.
- Check: both exit 0; every PRD `FR-N` is represented; requirements phase is
  approved (`csdd spec status <feature>`).
- If blocked: if a PRD requirement cannot be expressed as testable EARS, it was
  not testable in the PRD — send it back to `discovery-prd`, do not invent.

## Completion Criteria
- [ ] `product.md` (and `tech.md` if relevant) reflect the PRD; `csdd steering validate` passes.
- [ ] `specs/<feature>/` exists with requirements generated and filled in EARS.
- [ ] `csdd spec validate <feature>` exits 0 and the requirements phase is approved.
- [ ] Every PRD `FR-N` traces to at least one EARS requirement.
- [ ] The user is pointed to `wf:development` / `dev-architecture` as next.

## References

- Consult `.claude/rules/ears-format.md` for EARS syntax while translating requirements.
